// THE SEAM. This file is the one place where "what shape are the clouds" is answered, and everything
// downstream of it — the march, the lighting, the temporal accumulation, the composite — is written
// against `CloudFieldSample` and never against a producer.
//
// That matters because the programme has two producers by design (Docs/Clouds/ANALYSIS_APPROACH.md §4):
//
//   P  PROCEDURAL  computed from the component's parameters. Infinite, needs no content, and is the
//                  right tool for the far field, where a cloud's angular size is already below what the
//                  march can resolve and vertical structure is invisible in principle.
//   A  AUTHORED    sampled from voxel volumes an artist sculpted. Arbitrary silhouettes, tunnels, a cloud
//                  shaped like anything at all — the near field and the hero clouds.
//
// BOTH EXIST NOW. A joins at the bottom of this file as a `max` of the two profiles with the winner's
// detail type and density scale — one function, one level up from the `max` the four species already
// take — and NOTHING above this file changed when it landed: the march, the shadow map, the lighting,
// the temporal resolve and the composite are the text they were, because they were written against
// `CloudFieldSample` and never against a producer. The seam was written first in Э0 precisely so that
// the second producer would be a plug rather than a rewrite, and that is what it turned out to be.
//
// THE MEANING OF `Profile` IS LOAD-BEARING and is Nubis's, not a density: it is how deep inside the body
// of the cloud a point is, 0 at the surface and 1 in the core (deck p.85, where it is generated from a
// signed distance field for exactly this gradient). Six different calculations end up hanging off it —
// the silhouette, the frequency blend of the wispy noise, the frequency blend of the billowy noise, the
// multiple-scattering volume, the ambient term, and the early-out — which is why it is worth carrying a
// quantity that means "depth inside" rather than the density a naive implementation would store.
//
// HOW THE DATA ARRIVES. Through two macros, never through a sampler declared here:
//
//     CLOUD_SAMPLE_NOISE( vec3 texturePosition )   ->  vec4, the four channels of the noise volume
//     CLOUD_SAMPLE_PROFILE( vec2 uv )              ->  vec4, the vertical profile table — ONE CHANNEL PER
//                                                      SPECIES, in the order of the layer's slots
//     CLOUD_SAMPLE_AUTHORED( vec3 uvw )            ->  vec4, the four channels of a sculpted body
//
// The third arrives on the same terms as the first two, and the instance list it is addressed with comes
// from Common/CloudAuthored.glslh, which must have been included before this file.
//
// The march defines them as `texture()` fetches; the unit tests define the first as a call into the same
// CloudNoiseVolumeChannels that baked the volume and the second as a filtered read of the very table
// Graphic::CloudBuildProfileTable produced. This is the mechanism SkyScattering.glslh already uses for its
// LUT callbacks, and it exists because a sampler cannot appear in text that is also compiled as C++ —
// which is the only way the maths below gets a test at all. When the macros are undefined the structural
// half of this header still compiles, which is what lets a consumer include it for the types alone.
//
// Dialect rules as in Common/CloudNoise.glslh.

#ifndef CLOUD_FIELD_GLSLH
#define CLOUD_FIELD_GLSLH

// Requires Common/CloudNoise.glslh, Common/CloudGeometry.glslh and Common/CloudAuthored.glslh to have
// been included first. GLSL has no forward declarations, so include order is the caller's responsibility
// and is stated here rather than discovered as a compile error.

// HOW MANY KINDS OF CLOUD ONE SKY HOLDS AT ONCE. Four, and the number is the width of a texel rather than
// a preference: a species owns one CHANNEL of the profile table, which is Unreal's arrangement exactly
// (EpicDoc_CloudMaterial.md §2-3 fixes four types to R, G, B and A). Graphic::kCloudSpeciesSlots is the
// same constant on the C++ side. The LIBRARY on disk is not limited by this; a single LAYER is.
#define CLOUD_SPECIES_SLOTS 4

// What every producer returns and what the whole renderer consumes.
//
// THE LAST THREE ARE THE WINNING SPECIES', and they travel here rather than in the parameter block for a
// reason that only exists once a sky has more than one kind of cloud in it. What a cloud is MADE OF — how
// deeply its edge erodes, how much matter is in it, how opaque that matter is — is a property of the kind
// of cloud, so with four kinds in one sky it stops being a constant of the march and becomes a property of
// the SAMPLE. Carried from the species whose profile won the union, never blended: a blend of a
// stratocumulus and a congestus is a third thing that is neither, and the seam where two bodies meet is
// exactly where a floating average is visible as a smear of the wrong character.
struct CloudFieldSample
{
    float Profile;          // 0..1, depth inside the cloud body (see the header note)
    float DetailType;       // 0 = wispy, 1 = billowy — selects which erosion the up-rez applies
    float DensityScale;     // per-sample multiplier applied AFTER erosion (deck p.118)
    float DetailFactor;     // the winner's multiplier on the layer's erosion depth
    float ExtinctionFactor; // the winner's multiplier on the layer's extinction
};

// Everything the procedural producer needs, unpacked from the GPU block by Common/CloudParams.glslh.
// Distances are KILOMETRES, matching Common/CloudGeometry.glslh.
struct CloudFieldParams
{
    float WeatherTileKm;    // horizontal period of the LAYER's coverage field; a species scales it
    float Coverage;         // 0 = clear, 1 = solid
    float CoverageContrast; // sharpness of the cloud/clear transition; >1 narrows the band
    float DetailTileKm;     // period of the erosion field
    float DetailStrength;   // how deeply the erosion cuts into the profile, for the LAYER
    float DensityScale;     // the LAYER's density multiplier; the species' own is folded in per sample
    int   SpeciesCount;     // how many slots of the two arrays below are filled, 0..CLOUD_SPECIES_SLOTS

    // Per species k: x DetailCharacter, y DetailFactor, z DensityFactor, w ExtinctionFactor. Four numbers
    // that describe what that kind of cloud is made of, and all four are read.
    vec4 SpeciesEdge[CLOUD_SPECIES_SLOTS];

    // Per species k: the two HORIZONTAL basis vectors of that species' own placement field, in the world
    // XZ plane and already carrying its scale and its anisotropy. xy is the along-wind axis, zw the
    // across-wind one; both are divided by WeatherTileKm here rather than on the CPU so that the layer's
    // calibrated tile stays one number with one consumer.
    //
    // TWO VECTORS AND NOT A SCALE PLUS AN ANGLE, because the rotation into the wind's frame is the same
    // arithmetic for every sample and depends on nothing the march knows — so it is done once, on the
    // CPU, where PackCloudParams already forms every other product of a type's factor with a layer's
    // setting.
    vec4 SpeciesPlacement[CLOUD_SPECIES_SLOTS];

    vec3 WindOffsetKm; // accumulated wind displacement, subtracted from the sample position
};

// WHERE EACH SPECIES' PLACEMENT FIELD IS READ FROM, and it is a displacement in TEXTURE space rather than
// in kilometres so that it does not move when the scale does.
//
// Two species that happen to share a placement scale would otherwise read the identical field and land
// their patches on top of each other exactly — which is the one arrangement a set of independent fields
// must not produce, because it is indistinguishable from the partition D-14 rejects. The volume is
// periodic with period 1, so any offset that is not a small rational is a different realization of the
// same statistics; these are multiples of three mutually irrational-ish constants, and slot 0 gets zero so
// that a layer carrying ONE species reads the field T1 read, texel for texel.
vec3 CloudSpeciesPlacementOffset( int slot )
{
    float f = float( slot );
    return vec3( f * 137.13f, f * 61.71f, f * 211.37f );
}

// There are deliberately NO SEEDS AND NO OCTAVE COUNTS here. Both are properties of the BAKE — they
// decide what is in the volume, not how it is read — so carrying them into the march would be four
// parameters that every sample copies and no sample uses. They live in the bake pass's push constant,
// which is the only place they are read.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The anisotropy of each octave. THREE VECTORS, NOT ONE SCALAR.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// What stood here was a single number, `verticalTile = tile / 2.2`, taken from the ratio of Unreal's
// Noise1 vertical coefficient to its horizontal one. Three things were wrong with one number, and all
// three are visible in the graph (ShapeModel.md §11.2, coefficients at L1161 / L1087 / L1124):
//
//   * the two HORIZONTAL axes are not equal either — Noise1 is (4.167, 4.444), so the field is stretched
//     6.65 % along one of them, and a cloud field with two identical horizontal periods reads as being on
//     a square grid in a way a slightly rectangular one does not;
//   * the vertical ratio is DIFFERENT FOR EVERY OCTAVE — 2.18 for the shape, 1.25 for the distortion,
//     0.83 for the third;
//   * one of them is INVERTED. Noise3's vertical coefficient is BELOW its horizontal one, so that octave
//     is stretched UPWARD rather than flattened. A single scalar cannot express a ratio that is above one
//     for one octave and below one for another, whatever value it takes.
//
// The numbers below are those coefficients, normalised so the first horizontal axis is 1, and they are
// assigned to our octaves BY ROLE wherever a role exists: Noise1 is Unreal's base shape and ours is the
// coverage field; Noise2 is described by Epic as the distortion of Noise1 and ours is the domain warp
// below. Our erosion octave has no counterpart in the graph at all — UE has no Nubis-style erosion — so
// it takes the remaining octave, and the fact that the remaining one is the INVERTED one is a small piece
// of luck: wisps that are taller than they are wide is what a torn cloud edge looks like.
//
// Y IS UP HERE AND Z IS UP IN UNREAL, so the middle number of each triple is Unreal's third.
#define CLOUD_COVERAGE_FREQ_X 1.0f
#define CLOUD_COVERAGE_FREQ_Y 2.182f // 9.091 / 4.167
#define CLOUD_COVERAGE_FREQ_Z 1.0665f // 4.444 / 4.167

#define CLOUD_DETAIL_FREQ_X 1.0f
#define CLOUD_DETAIL_FREQ_Y 0.833f // 25 / 30 — BELOW one: this octave is stretched upward
#define CLOUD_DETAIL_FREQ_Z 1.333f // 40 / 30

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE HEIGHT-DEPENDENT DOMAIN WARP IS NOT HERE, AND THAT IS A MEASURED DECISION
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Unreal displaces the base octave's COORDINATES by a second noise, on a vector that depends on the height
// in the layer: horizontally the shove grows from 0.05 at the base to 1 at the top, vertically it is a
// parabola that vanishes mid-layer (ShapeModel.md §10, and Epic's own words for the parameter are
// "controls distortion amount and scale for Noise1"). It is the mechanism by which a two-dimensional
// pattern stops being extruded upward into a column, and it was built here, measured, and taken out again.
//
// WHAT WAS MEASURED. Clouds_Demo, camera 0,200,0, three elevations x two azimuths, ImageStat contrast over
// the full width and the top 71.9% of the frame, at three settings of the warp — off, the proposed default
// and the slider's maximum:
//
//     zenith away   0.483 / 0.475 / 0.467      mid away   0.491 / 0.493 / 0.477
//     zenith sun    0.497 / 0.497 / 0.509      mid sun    0.385 / 0.385 / 0.403
//     horizon away  0.308 / 0.304 / 0.295      horizon sun 0.367 / 0.365 / 0.369
//
// The largest movement across the WHOLE travel of the control is 0.018 of contrast and it has no
// consistent sign; mean luminance moves by less than 0.004. For scale, replacing the analytic profile with
// the table moved the same numbers by up to 0.08, and the three species differ from one another by more
// than 0.2.
//
// WHY IT BUYS NOTHING HERE, and this is not a failure of the technique. Unreal reads its placement pattern
// as a two-dimensional texture, so without the warp its silhouette genuinely IS an extrusion and the warp
// is the only three-dimensionality it has. Our coverage field is sampled in FULL 3D (see the long note on
// the producer below), so the field already differs at every altitude by more than the warp displaces it —
// the warp is a correlated shear on top of a decorrelation that has already happened. Two solutions to one
// problem, and we were already carrying the other one.
//
// It cost one extra fetch of the volume per field sample, in the march AND in every light-march sample.
// Paid for a change nobody can see, that is the most expensive line in the file.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The vertical profile: A TABLE, and this is the read side of it
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Deck p.19 composes the shape as `dimensional_profile = vertical_profile * cloud_coverage` — a PRODUCT,
// and the multiplication is the point: the coverage field owns WHERE a cloud is, the vertical profile owns
// its silhouette, and neither has to encode the other. That has not changed. What changed is what the
// vertical profile IS.
//
// It was `f(heightFraction, cloudType)` — one analytic curve with two interpolated constants — and it is
// now a two-dimensional table, `f(height in the envelope, VALUE OF THE PLACEMENT PATTERN HERE)`, which is
// Unreal's arrangement byte for byte (ShapeModel.md §3: `ProfileObject.SampleLevel(float2(
// NormAltitudeInLayer, L_i))`) and decision D-13. Two things follow that no curve delivers:
//
//   * a species is LOW AND FLAT at the rim of a patch and a TOWER in its middle, because the second axis
//     is how deep inside the patch this column is. Height that correlates with how much cloud is there,
//     rather than height sprinkled by a noise that knows nothing about the patch;
//   * a profile may have TWO humps. A cumulonimbus anvil is a lobe of cloud at the tropopause with a gap
//     between it and the tower that fed it, and `baseRamp * topRamp` has exactly one maximum for any
//     choice of constants.
//
// The table is generated, not authored — Graphic::CloudBuildProfileTable evaluates a parametric curve per
// species into 256 x 64 texels — so a scene nobody has art-directed still has a profile. The curve did not
// die; it moved to the other side of the texture.
//
// THE AXES. U is the height fraction in the envelope, which is the species' OWN [base, top] in kilometres
// (the envelope is computed from the species by Graphic::PackCloudParams, never authored). V is the
// pattern. Both are clamped to the texel centres because every sampler in this engine is REPEAT: without
// the clamp a height fraction of 1 wraps to the bottom row and the top of the layer grows the cloud's own
// base back on top of itself.
#define CLOUD_PROFILE_TABLE_ALTITUDE_TEXELS 256.0f
#define CLOUD_PROFILE_TABLE_PATTERN_TEXELS  64.0f

#ifdef CLOUD_SAMPLE_PROFILE
// ALL FOUR SPECIES IN ONE FETCH, which is the whole economy of putting a species on a channel. The march
// asks "which kinds of cloud can be here at all" once, and a 64 KiB table that is certainly in cache
// answers for four of them together.
vec4 CloudSampleProfileTableAll( float heightFraction, float pattern )
{
    float halfU = 0.5f / CLOUD_PROFILE_TABLE_ALTITUDE_TEXELS;
    float halfV = 0.5f / CLOUD_PROFILE_TABLE_PATTERN_TEXELS;

    float u = clamp( heightFraction, halfU, 1.0f - halfU );
    float v = clamp( pattern, halfV, 1.0f - halfV );

    vec4 profiles = CLOUD_SAMPLE_PROFILE( vec2( u, v ) );

    return vec4( clamp( profiles.x, 0.0f, 1.0f ), clamp( profiles.y, 0.0f, 1.0f ),
                 clamp( profiles.z, 0.0f, 1.0f ), clamp( profiles.w, 0.0f, 1.0f ) );
}

// One species, by slot. The tests read the table this way and so does anything that wants a single kind.
float CloudSampleProfileTable( float heightFraction, float pattern, int slot )
{
    return CloudSampleProfileTableAll( heightFraction, pattern )[slot];
}
#endif // CLOUD_SAMPLE_PROFILE

#if defined( CLOUD_SAMPLE_NOISE ) && defined( CLOUD_SAMPLE_PROFILE ) && defined( CLOUD_SAMPLE_AUTHORED )

// The procedural producer.
//
// THE BASE SHAPE IS SAMPLED IN FULL 3D, and that correction is the single most important line in this
// file. It was first written with y dropped — the coverage field read as a horizontal plane and extruded
// vertically by the profile — and the result is not a subtle loss of quality: every cloud becomes a
// literal vertical column, and from any angle other than straight up you see its SIDE WALL. In motion
// the sky reads as a row of hanging curtains with flat bottoms and hard vertical edges. It is invisible
// at the zenith, where you look along the columns, which is exactly how it survived the first round of
// checks.
//
// The published construction (Schneider, SIGGRAPH 2015) has it the other way round from the start: a 3D
// noise supplies the SILHOUETTE, and the weather map only decides where a cloud is allowed to be. That is
// what this now does.
//
// The vertical axis is sampled at a higher frequency than the horizontal, because a cloud is wider than
// it is tall — and by a DIFFERENT factor per axis and per octave, see the frequency triples above.

// One species' profile at one point: the coverage threshold, the pattern axis and the table read, exactly
// as T1 wrote them for the single species this used to have. Split out rather than inlined because it is
// the half of the producer that is the SAME for every species — only the field it is handed differs — and
// four copies of a threshold is four places for a constant to drift.
float CloudSpeciesProfile( CloudFieldParams params, vec4 noise, float heightFraction, int slot )
{
    // THE COVERAGE FIELD IS THE BILLOWY PAIR, AND WHICH PAIR IT IS DECIDES WHETHER THERE ARE CLOUDS.
    //
    // This read the WISPY pair — channels R and G, low- and high-frequency Curly-Alligator — and that is
    // not a tuning error, it is a category error, and it has one visible symptom: the sky comes out as a
    // COBWEB. Curly-Alligator is `1 - Alligator`, and the Alligator's zero set is the seams BETWEEN its
    // cells (Common/CloudNoise.glslh: the field is `best - second`, which vanishes where two feature points
    // contribute equally). Inverted, those seams are the field's maxima — so thresholding it near the top,
    // which is exactly what a coverage of 0.25 does, selects the seams and nothing else. What survives is a
    // net of filaments, and the deck says as much about what that channel is FOR: "nice web like shapes"
    // (p.95), the wispy DETAIL, never the shape.
    //
    // The billowy pair is the other half of the same volume and it is the complementary set: the Alligator
    // itself, whose maxima are the feature points. Thresholding it near the top selects the LOBES — "the
    // lobes a convective cloud is made of", which is what the channel is labelled in
    // Engine/Assets/CloudNoiseVolume.hpp — and one lobe crossed by a view ray is hundreds of metres of
    // cloud instead of tens. Measured on the shipped scene at three elevations: the median cloud crossing
    // along a view ray went from 60 / 70 / 150 m to 430 / 550 / 920 m, against a march step of 221 / 233 /
    // 234 m. Same two weights, the other pair.
    //
    // WHAT IT REALLY WANTS IS NOT A CHANNEL OF THIS VOLUME AT ALL, and that has to be said here rather than
    // discovered again. In Nubis Cubed the coverage comes from a weather map and the shape from MODELLING
    // volumes an artist sculpts; this four-channel volume is the deck's DETAIL noise and nothing in it was
    // designed to be a silhouette. That producer is the seam's slot A at the top of this file, and until it
    // exists the coverage borrows the only lobe-shaped field the tree has. The borrow is legitimate — the
    // Alligator's lacunarity is what the deck chose it for, and a range of lobe sizes with creases between
    // them is a cumulus field — but it is a borrow, and slot A is where it is repaid.
    //
    // The weights are the coarse octave owning the island and the finer one roughening its edge, and they
    // sum to one so that `Coverage` keeps meaning the same thing whatever the noise is doing locally.
    float coarseWeight = 0.65f;
    float fineWeight   = 0.35f;
    float blended      = noise.z * coarseWeight + noise.w * fineWeight;

    // AND THEN THE BLEND IS TURNED INTO A QUANTILE, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES THE SLIDER SURVIVE A NEW VOLUME.
    //
    // The threshold below sweeps the whole of [0, 1] and therefore assumes the field is spread over the
    // whole of [0, 1]. It is not, and it never was. A weighted mean of two independent fields is NARROWER
    // than either — variances add as the square of the weights, so this blend's standard deviation is
    // sqrt(0.65^2 + 0.35^2) = 0.738 of one channel's — and the raw blend measures
    // p05 0.273 / p50 0.517 / p95 0.786. At the shipped Coverage of 0.24 the threshold lands at 0.79, ABOVE
    // the blend's own 95th percentile: a setting documented as scattered cumulus was selecting the top
    // twentieth of the field, and the zenith came out nearly empty. The threshold's span against the
    // field's span — 1.0 of slider travel against 0.51 of field — is this programme's classic relation
    // defect, and it was invisible while the field was the wispy pair, because thresholding a web produces
    // SOMETHING at any level.
    //
    // The fix is not a nicer constant, it is a change of what the field MEANS: the smoothstep below is the
    // blend's own cumulative distribution, so `field` stops being a value and becomes the QUANTILE of that
    // value — the fraction of the sky whose coverage field is below this point. `Coverage` then addresses a
    // fraction of sky directly, and it does so for ANY volume the generator produces, because every
    // Alligator channel leaves it with the same distribution whatever seed and periods it was baked with.
    // THAT IS THE PROPERTY T2 NEEDS: the slot now takes an artist's asset, and a slider whose meaning moves
    // with the asset dropped into it is a defect the asset feature itself would have introduced. It already
    // happened once without an asset in sight — the component's coverage table shifted five points of
    // slider when the noise changed from Perlin-Worley to Alligator, and that whole class of shift is what
    // this removes.
    //
    // 0.32 IS MEASURED, exactly like CLOUD_ALLIGATOR_MEDIAN_SHAPE in Common/CloudNoise.glslh and for the
    // same reason: it is the half-width at which the mapped field's quantiles equal its own values. The
    // suite prints them and asserts it — 0.057 / 0.287 / 0.540 / 0.791 / 0.992 against the ideal
    // 0.05 / 0.25 / 0.50 / 0.75 / 0.95 — so a change to the noise or to the weights fails here rather than
    // in a frame six weeks later. Being monotone it moves no level set: the islands are exactly where the
    // Alligator put them and only their labels change.
    //
    // Both ends stay exact. The mapped field reaches 1, and at Coverage 0 the band starts past 1; it
    // reaches 0, and at Coverage 1 the band ends below 0.
    float distributionHalfWidth = 0.32f;
    float field = smoothstep( 0.5f - distributionHalfWidth, 0.5f + distributionHalfWidth, blended );

    // Coverage as a SOFT MASK around a threshold, not a remap of the whole range above it.
    //
    // The remap was the obvious form and it is quietly wrong. `remap(field, 1 - Coverage, 1, 0, 1)`
    // rescales everything above the threshold across the full output range, so at a coverage of 0.55 the
    // MEDIAN of the field lands at 0.09 — a value that survives no erosion at all. The result was a sky
    // uniformly filled with veil: mathematically there was cloud almost everywhere, and visibly there was
    // cloud nowhere. Only the top per cent of the field ever produced a body.
    //
    // A smoothstep centred on the threshold behaves the way a weather map has to: most of the sky is
    // decisively inside a cloud or decisively outside one, with a transition band whose WIDTH is the
    // contrast. That is what puts an edge on an island and clear blue between islands.
    // COVERAGE 0 MUST MEAN A CLEAR SKY, and the obvious threshold does not deliver it. Written as
    // `1 - Coverage`, the smoothstep at Coverage 0 still spans 1 +- halfWidth, so every part of the field
    // above 0.82 produced cloud: measured, forty per cent of columns were still touched at a setting whose
    // documented meaning is "clear". The same error eats the bottom of the slider, where the interesting
    // settings are.
    //
    // Interpolating the threshold across the FULL span instead makes both ends exact: at 0 the band starts
    // at 1.0, which the field never exceeds, and at 1 it ends at 0.0, which the field never falls below.
    float halfWidth = 0.06f / max( params.CoverageContrast, 0.05f );
    // The epsilon is float arithmetic, not slack. At Coverage 0 the band's lower edge is
    // `threshold - halfWidth`, and 1.06f - 0.06f evaluates to 0.99999994 rather than to 1 — so a field
    // value of exactly 1, which the clamp above produces, lands a hair INSIDE the band and one column in a
    // thousand grows a cloud in a sky the artist asked to be empty. Pushing both ends a millimetre past
    // the field's range makes "clear" and "solid" exact rather than nearly exact.
    float threshold = mix( 1.0f + halfWidth + 1e-3f, -halfWidth - 1e-3f, clamp( params.Coverage, 0.0f, 1.0f ) );

    float coverage = smoothstep( threshold - halfWidth, threshold + halfWidth, field );

    // THE PATTERN AXIS OF THE PROFILE TABLE: how deep inside the patch this column is, measured from the
    // level at which cloud begins.
    //
    // It is NOT the raw field. The threshold sweeps the whole of [0, 1] with the slider, so at a coverage
    // of 0.15 every column that has any cloud in it has a field between 0.79 and 1 — a fifth of the axis —
    // and four fifths of the table would never be read. Measuring from the band's lower edge instead
    // spends the whole axis at every setting: 0 exactly where the island begins and 1 at the field's own
    // maximum. That is what makes "flat at the rim, a tower in the middle" a property of the island rather
    // than a property of the slider.
    //
    // It is not `coverage` either. The transition band is 0.06 wide and the smoothstep across it saturates
    // within a couple of per cent of the island's edge, so the pattern would be 1 almost everywhere and
    // every cloud would be full height again.
    float patternFloor = threshold - halfWidth;
    float pattern      = clamp( ( field - patternFloor ) / max( 1.0f - patternFloor, 1e-3f ), 0.0f, 1.0f );

    float vertical = CloudSampleProfileTable( heightFraction, pattern, slot );

    return clamp( vertical * coverage, 0.0f, 1.0f );
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// FOUR SPECIES, FOUR INDEPENDENT PLACEMENT FIELDS, JOINED BY `max`
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// WHAT WAS PLANNED AND WHY IT WAS WRONG. The first written form of this was a PARTITION: one placement
// field, sliced between the species by smoothsteps, weights summing to one. That construction forbids the
// only frame this phase exists to produce — a low stratocumulus deck with a congestus tower standing in it
// and partly behind it — because weights that sum to one mean no point of the sky can belong to two kinds
// of cloud at once. The plan recorded the frame and the rule that makes it unreachable on the same page
// (PLAN_CLOUD_TYPES.md §3a).
//
// WHAT IT IS INSTEAD (decision D-14). A species' placement field is its OWN field: its own scale, its own
// anisotropy, its own realization of the noise. Nothing is normalized across species, nothing sums to
// anything, and two species overlapping is the ordinary case rather than the impossible one. Unreal says
// the same thing in one line — `Layout_CloudGlobalPattern` "defines the world location for each type of
// cloud where EACH CHANNEL describes a different cloud type", with `Layout_CloudPerTypeScale` beside it.
//
// WHY THE JOIN IS STILL `max`. Profile means "how deep inside a body of cloud this point is", not
// "density", so the deeper of two answers is the answer and the order the species were listed in cannot
// matter. Adding them would make an overlap denser than either body — the same argument, from the same
// paragraph, that joins the seam's two producers (ANALYSIS_APPROACH.md §4.3) and joins a cumulonimbus'
// tower to its own anvil.
//
// WHERE THE FIELDS COME FROM, AND THE NUMBER THAT DECIDES IT. Each species reads the SAME pair of channels
// of the same volume — the billowy Alligator pair — at its own scale, its own anisotropy and its own
// offset. It does NOT read a different channel pair per species, and that is the one option a reader would
// expect and has to be argued away: the quantile map below is calibrated to the Alligator blend's
// distribution (p05 0.273 / p50 0.517 / p95 0.786, half-width 0.32), and the wispy pair is `1 - Alligator`
// with the complementary shape. A species reading it would answer the SAME `Coverage` setting with a
// different fraction of sky — which is precisely the defect the quantile map was introduced to remove, put
// back four-fold. A change of scale and offset leaves the distribution exactly where it is, because the
// field is statistically stationary; Desert/Tests/Engine/CloudField measures the four species' quantiles
// and asserts they agree.
//
// WHAT IT COSTS. One profile fetch decides which species are alive here; each live one then costs one 3D
// fetch and one more profile fetch. In the ordinary sky that is one species, and the cost is T1's. Where
// two bands overlap it is two. The four-species case is a sky nobody has authored yet.
CloudFieldSample CloudSampleProceduralField( CloudFieldParams params, float heightFraction, vec3 positionKm )
{
    CloudFieldSample result;
    result.Profile          = 0.0f;
    result.DetailType       = 0.0f;
    result.DensityScale     = 0.0f;
    result.DetailFactor     = 0.0f;
    result.ExtinctionFactor = 0.0f;

    // THE CHEAP REJECTION, and it is exact rather than conservative, and it now answers for four species
    // at once. The profile table's support grows monotonically with the pattern — a fuller patch is taller
    // and its anvil is more present, never less — so a height at which the pattern's own maximum yields
    // nothing yields nothing at any pattern. One 2D fetch, from a 64 KiB table that will be in cache, in
    // place of every 3D fetch below. A species whose band does not reach this altitude has a zero here and
    // costs nothing at all; so does an empty slot, whose channel was never written.
    vec4 alive = CloudSampleProfileTableAll( heightFraction, 1.0f );
    if ( max( max( alive.x, alive.y ), max( alive.z, alive.w ) ) <= 0.0f )
        return result;

    vec3 windPos = vec3( positionKm.x - params.WindOffsetKm.x, positionKm.y - params.WindOffsetKm.y,
                         positionKm.z - params.WindOffsetKm.z );

    float tile = max( params.WeatherTileKm, 1e-3f );

    int speciesCount = min( params.SpeciesCount, CLOUD_SPECIES_SLOTS );

    for ( int slot = 0; slot < speciesCount; ++slot )
    {
        if ( alive[slot] <= 0.0f )
            continue;

        // THIS SPECIES' OWN FRAME. The two basis vectors already carry its scale and its stretch along the
        // wind; dividing by the layer's tile here is what keeps Max View Distance / Weather Tile Size —
        // the calibrated pair of CALIBRATION.md §4 — a relation between two numbers the artist can see.
        vec4 basis      = params.SpeciesPlacement[slot];
        vec2 alongAxis  = vec2( basis.x, basis.y );
        vec2 acrossAxis = vec2( basis.z, basis.w );
        vec2 horizontal = vec2( windPos.x, windPos.z );

        // The VERTICAL frequency follows the ACROSS-wind axis and not the along-wind one, because a
        // stretch along the wind is a horizontal shear: a fibrous cirrus is drawn out downwind and is no
        // taller for it. Derived from the across axis' length rather than sent as a fifth number, so the
        // two cannot disagree.
        float verticalFreq = CLOUD_COVERAGE_FREQ_Y * length( acrossAxis ) / CLOUD_COVERAGE_FREQ_Z;

        vec3 coveragePos = vec3( dot( horizontal, alongAxis ) / tile, windPos.y * verticalFreq / tile,
                                 dot( horizontal, acrossAxis ) / tile );

        coveragePos = coveragePos + CloudSpeciesPlacementOffset( slot );

        vec4 noise = CLOUD_SAMPLE_NOISE( coveragePos );

        float speciesProfile = CloudSpeciesProfile( params, noise, heightFraction, slot );
        if ( speciesProfile <= result.Profile )
            continue;

        // THE WINNER TAKES ALL FOUR, and does not share any of them. See the note on CloudFieldSample.
        vec4 edge = params.SpeciesEdge[slot];

        result.Profile          = speciesProfile;
        result.DetailType       = clamp( edge.x, 0.0f, 1.0f );
        result.DetailFactor     = max( edge.y, 0.0f );
        result.DensityScale     = max( params.DensityScale, 0.0f ) * max( edge.z, 0.0f );
        result.ExtinctionFactor = max( edge.w, 0.0f );
    }

    return result;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// SLOT A — THE AUTHORED PRODUCER
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// What slot A answers with: a field sample AND how much of the procedural field this point's instances
// are suppressing. Two values because the cutout is not a property of the sample — it decides what the
// OTHER producer is allowed to contribute — and this dialect has no `out` parameters, deliberately.
struct CloudAuthoredResult
{
    CloudFieldSample Field;
    float            Cutout; // 0 = the procedural field stands, 1 = it is entirely suppressed here
};

// Sample every hero cloud whose bounds contain this point, and union them.
//
// THE COST IS THE WHOLE DESIGN OF THIS FUNCTION, because it is called once per march step AND once per
// SHADOW-RAY step, which is about thirty times more often again. Three gates in order of price:
//
//   1. THE COUNT. A scene with no hero clouds does not enter the loop at all. That is what makes the
//      producer cost zero when it is absent rather than almost zero (ANALYSIS_APPROACH.md §4.4). The
//      frames measure it: five of the protocol's six points render BYTE FOR BYTE the file they rendered
//      before this producer existed, and the sixth moves 199 pixels of 980 480 by one 8-bit level —
//      which is not this loop but the recompiled SPIR-V scheduling the PROCEDURAL producer's floating
//      point differently, because the file it lives in changed. Docs/Clouds/CALIBRATION.md §A0.
//   2. THE AXIS-ALIGNED BOX, six compares. It is a superset of the instance's real bounds and it is here
//      because it is four times cheaper than the map that follows.
//   3. THE EXACT BOX, after the map. A rotated instance's AABB contains up to 3.4x its volume, and a
//      point in that difference must not fetch: every sampler in this engine is REPEAT, so a coordinate
//      outside [0, 1] would fetch the far side of the body and grow a mirror of its own flank beside it.
//
// The union rule is the SPECIES' RULE, one level up (§4.3): profile means "how deep inside a body", so
// the deeper answer is the answer, and the winner's material numbers travel with it rather than being
// blended — a blend of two bodies' detail character is a third character that is neither, and the seam
// between them is exactly where a floating average shows as a smear.
CloudAuthoredResult CloudSampleAuthoredField( CloudFieldParams params, vec3 positionKm )
{
    CloudAuthoredResult result;
    result.Field.Profile          = 0.0f;
    result.Field.DetailType       = 0.0f;
    result.Field.DensityScale     = 0.0f;
    result.Field.DetailFactor     = 0.0f;
    result.Field.ExtinctionFactor = 0.0f;
    result.Cutout                 = 0.0f;

    int count = CLOUD_AUTHORED_COUNT;

    for ( int i = 0; i < count; ++i )
    {
        CloudAuthoredInstance instance = CLOUD_AUTHORED_INSTANCE( i );

        if ( !CloudAuthoredInBounds( instance, positionKm ) )
            continue;

        vec3 uvw = CloudAuthoredLocalUvw( instance, positionKm );
        if ( !CloudAuthoredInVolume( uvw ) )
            continue;

        vec4 voxel = CLOUD_SAMPLE_AUTHORED( CloudAuthoredClampUvw( uvw ) );

        float strength = instance.BoundsMin.w;

        // THE CUTOUT UNIONS ACROSS INSTANCES AND IS NOT TIED TO THE WINNER. Two hero clouds standing in
        // each other's boxes each suppress the procedural field over their own body, and the procedural
        // field has to be gone from BOTH — taking only the winner's would let a blob grow through the
        // one that lost by a hair.
        //
        // It is the volume's ENVELOPE channel and not the box, which is the difference between a hero
        // cloud that pushes the deck away from its own skin and one that cuts a rectangular hole in the
        // sky around itself. The envelope is the body dilated by the bake's margin, so it is above zero
        // wherever the profile is and extends past it: a tunnel through the body narrower than that
        // margin is inside the envelope and shows sky, which is what a tunnel is for.
        result.Cutout = max( result.Cutout, voxel.w * instance.BoundsMax.w );

        float profile = voxel.x * strength;
        if ( profile <= result.Field.Profile )
            continue;

        result.Field.Profile      = profile;
        result.Field.DetailType   = clamp( voxel.y, 0.0f, 1.0f );
        result.Field.DetailFactor = instance.Row0.w;
        // The layer's density, this body's factor and the volume's own per-voxel scale, in that order —
        // the same three-level composition the procedural producer performs, so a Density Scale of 1
        // means the same thing on both sides of the seam.
        result.Field.DensityScale     = max( params.DensityScale, 0.0f ) * instance.Row1.w * voxel.z;
        result.Field.ExtinctionFactor = instance.Row2.w;
    }

    return result;
}

// THE SEAM ITSELF. One producer that unions up to four species, one that unions up to four sculpted
// bodies, and the max of the two — the same `max`, one level up, which is what both unions below it were
// written to make obvious.
//
// THE AUTHORED SIDE GOES FIRST, and not because either order changes the union: `max` is commutative and
// Desert/Tests/Engine/CloudAuthored asserts it on the whole sample. It goes first because it decides
// whether the procedural side is computed AT ALL. Inside a hero cloud the cutout is 1, the procedural
// field is entirely suppressed there, and computing it would be a 3D fetch, a profile fetch and a
// quantile map thrown away — so the branch below is a saving in exactly the place a hero cloud makes the
// frame most expensive.
CloudFieldSample SampleCloudField( CloudFieldParams params, float heightFraction, vec3 positionKm )
{
    CloudAuthoredResult authored = CloudSampleAuthoredField( params, positionKm );

    CloudFieldSample result;
    result.Profile          = 0.0f;
    result.DetailType       = 0.0f;
    result.DensityScale     = 0.0f;
    result.DetailFactor     = 0.0f;
    result.ExtinctionFactor = 0.0f;

    if ( authored.Cutout < 1.0f )
    {
        result = CloudSampleProceduralField( params, heightFraction, positionKm );

        // THE CUTOUT SCALES THE PROFILE RATHER THAN THRESHOLDING IT, so the procedural field fades out
        // over the envelope's own margin instead of ending at a surface. A hard test would put a visible
        // edge in the deck at the exact distance the envelope reaches, and that edge would be a shape
        // nobody authored — the bake's margin — drawn in somebody else's cloud.
        result.Profile = result.Profile * ( 1.0f - authored.Cutout );
    }

    if ( authored.Field.Profile > result.Profile )
        result = authored.Field;

    return result;
}

// The up-rez: turn the low-frequency profile into something with a cloud's edge.
//
// The erosion is a REMAP, not a subtraction (deck p.120, `ValueErosion`). The difference shows at the
// core: subtracting noise thins a dense cloud as much as a wispy one, so a cumulus turns translucent in
// the middle, while remapping leaves anything already deep inside the body at full density and only cuts
// where the profile was already falling off.
//
// `DetailType` selects between the volume's two CHARACTERS — wispy and billowy — and each of them is
// itself a frequency blend driven by the profile. That is the deck's composite on p.122 in full rather
// than the two-channel half of it that stood here, and the difference is that the erosion's frequency now
// follows how deep inside the body the sample is instead of being constant through the whole cloud.
float CloudSampleDensity( CloudFieldParams params, CloudFieldSample field, vec3 positionKm )
{
    if ( field.Profile <= 0.0f )
        return 0.0f;

    vec3 windPos = vec3( positionKm.x - params.WindOffsetKm.x, positionKm.y - params.WindOffsetKm.y,
                         positionKm.z - params.WindOffsetKm.z );

    // ANISOTROPIC, and it was not. The erosion was the one octave here still sampled as a cube, which
    // gives a torn cloud edge the proportions of gravel; the graph has anisotropy on all three of its
    // octaves. This one takes the INVERTED triple — vertical period longer than horizontal — so the wisps
    // it cuts are taller than they are wide, which is what a shred of cloud being carried upward looks
    // like.
    float tile      = max( params.DetailTileKm, 1e-4f );
    vec3  detailPos = vec3( windPos.x * CLOUD_DETAIL_FREQ_X / tile, windPos.y * CLOUD_DETAIL_FREQ_Y / tile,
                            windPos.z * CLOUD_DETAIL_FREQ_Z / tile );

    vec4 noise = CLOUD_SAMPLE_NOISE( detailPos );

    // THE DECK'S OWN COMPOSITE (p.122), and it uses all four channels rather than two of them.
    //
    // Each character of erosion is a blend of ITS OWN pair across frequency, and the blend is driven by the
    // profile — deep inside the body the coarse octave, out at the edge the fine one, which is the
    // frequency-follows-depth relation the deck states on p.101 and p.105. The wispy end blends linearly;
    // the billowy end blends on the fourth root, so a billowy cloud reaches its fine octave much earlier
    // and its edge breaks into lobes rather than fading into one smooth frequency.
    //
    // Only THEN do the two characters mix, on DetailType. Written the other way round — pick a channel by
    // type and never blend by depth — the erosion has one frequency everywhere inside a cloud, which is
    // what the two-channel version this replaces did.
    float wispy   = mix( noise.x, noise.y, field.Profile );
    float billowy = mix( noise.z, noise.w, pow( field.Profile, 0.25f ) );

    float detailField = clamp( mix( wispy, billowy, clamp( field.DetailType, 0.0f, 1.0f ) ), 0.0f, 1.0f );

    // THE EROSION IS WEIGHTED BY DEPTH, and this is the difference between clouds and fog.
    //
    // Applied uniformly, the erosion subtracts as much from the core of a cloud as from its edge — and
    // since the profile is only high in a thin band anyway, a strength of 0.5 removes most of the layer
    // and what survives is a translucent veil. That is not a tuning failure: a cut of fixed depth into a
    // field whose typical value is small removes the field.
    //
    // Weighting by (1 - Profile) puts the whole cut where the cloud is already ending. The core, which is
    // deep inside the body, keeps its density and stays solid; the edge, which is barely inside it, is
    // eaten into billows and wisps. It is the same principle the deck states as a frequency blend driven
    // by the dimensional profile (p.101, p.105) — the profile decides how much detail belongs here — and
    // it is why that field means "depth inside the body" rather than "density".
    // THE DEPTH OF THE CUT IS THE WINNING SPECIES', multiplied into the layer's own strength here rather
    // than folded together on the CPU as it was while a layer had one kind of cloud in it. A lenticular
    // has to stay a smooth lens and a cirrus has to stay shredded when the two are in the same sky, and a
    // single product formed once could only describe one of them.
    float edgeWeight = 1.0f - clamp( field.Profile, 0.0f, 1.0f );
    float erosion =
         detailField * clamp( params.DetailStrength * max( field.DetailFactor, 0.0f ), 0.0f, 1.0f ) * edgeWeight;

    float density = CloudRemap( field.Profile, erosion, 1.0f, 0.0f, 1.0f );

    return clamp( density * field.DensityScale, 0.0f, 1.0f );
}

#endif // CLOUD_SAMPLE_NOISE && CLOUD_SAMPLE_PROFILE

#endif // CLOUD_FIELD_GLSLH
